\section{Background}
Between 2007 and 2009, Dr. Kristen (alongside other researchers) at Palmer Station, Antarctica, collected data regarding body measurements of three penguin species: the Adelie, Chinstrap, and Gentoo. Body mass is a good indicator of an animal's condition, but flipper length can be much easier to measure, especially when out in the field. As such, this project asked "How strongly is flipper length related to body mass within each penguin species, and how much of the variation in body mass does flipper length explain?" This answer will determine whether it is worth relying on flipper length as the body mass predictor.

\section{Dataset Description}
The dataset recorded body-size measurements such as culmen (upper bill ridge) length and depth, flipper length and body mass, and information such as the sex and growth stage of the penguin (Horst 2022). Two of the 344 penguins had no related measurements logged and were therefore excluded, leaving $N=342$ penguins (151 Adelie, 68 Chinstrap, and 123 Gentoo). Before conducting any analyses, the three penguin species' flipper lengths and body masses were plotted separately to see if there are any noticeable differences, thus deciding whether they should be analyzed separately.

