Gentoo penguins are clearly larger than the other two species (flipper length about 217~mm versus 190--196~mm, body mass about 5100~g versus 3700~g), and all species differ in the spread of their measurements, so a single pooled analysis would mix the species size differences with the relationship we want to study. As such, each species will be analyzed separately.

\section{Method}
This penguin analysis was conducted in Python and run on Visual Studio Code. For each species, body mass $y$ was described in a linear regression function of flipper length $x$, using $y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x$, where $\beta_1$ is the slope (change in predicted body mass per additional millimeter of flipper) and $\beta_0$ is the intercept (the value at zero flipper length, which has no biological meaning, but it is needed to plot the line). The line was found through the "least squares" method, which is done by subtracting $\sum_i (y_i-\hat y_i)^2$, which is the squared differences between the actual measured masses $y_i$ and the predicted masses $\hat y_i$ that drawn from the line. The strength of this relationship is defined by the "Pearson correlation coefficient" $r$. Additionally, to determine how much of the variation in body mass is actually explained by flipper length, the coefficient of determination is considered $R^2 = 1-\mathrm{SS}_\mathrm{res}/\mathrm{SS}_\mathrm{tot}$ (where SSres is the residual sum of squares).

