In all three species, flipper length is positively related to body mass. In other words, penguins with longer flippers tend to be heavier! Every $p$-value is below $0.001$, so these observed relationships are very unlikely to be due to chance alone. The strength differs between species, from a moderate correlation for Adelie ($r=0.47$) to a stronger one for Chinstrap ($r=0.64$) and Gentoo ($r=0.70$). Flipper length explains about 22\,\% of the variation in body mass for Adelie, 41\,\% for Chinstrap and 49\,\% for Gentoo. Each additional millimeter of flipper corresponds to roughly 33, 35 and 55~g of extra predicted mass, respectively (as seen by the slope).

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\vspace{-0.2em}
Flipper length is a statistically significant predictor of body mass in all three species, but not a very precise one. A small $p$-value only suggests that any observed relationships are unlikely to be due to chance, whereas $R^2$ shows how well it predicts. For Gentoo, about half of the variation in body mass is explained by something other than flipper length, and for Adelie, almost 80\,\% is. Two penguins of the same species and flipper length can differ in mass by several hundred grams. Correlation does not equal causation: longer flippers probably just reflect overall body size and composition. The dataset's other variables, like sex, may contribute to differences. A future analysis could include several variables at once, for example by comparing means for 3 or more groups and determining which has the greatest impact through an ANOVA test. In conclusion, flipper length gives a quick and useful hint of a penguin's body mass, especially for Gentoo and Chinstrap penguins, but it cannot replace weighing the animal.



